        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Lunar Analog Feasibility Study Phase 2 (LAFSP2) started April 20, 2009 and ended on August 27, 2009.  The feasibility study was approved with a study sample size of twenty-three.  Eleven subjects completed the LAFS Phase 1 portion and only 8 completed the Phase 2 portion of the feasibility study.  The purpose of this research study was to determine the feasibility of a bed rest analog to simulate a lunar analog mission, provide preliminary data, and inform the study design of future protocols.  
<P>
This research study was performed at the Flight Analogs Research Unit (FARU) in the General Clinical Research Center (GCRC) at the University of Texas Medical Branch (UTMB) in Galveston, TX and managed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration‘s (NASA) Johnson Space Center (JSC) Life Science Research Laboratories. 
<P>
The subjects participated in screening activities prior to admittance into the study.  The pre-screening and pre-testing activities included the Human Test Subject Facility (HTSF) physical examination and a psychological evaluation which was completed at JSC.  This study constituted thirteen days of pre testing, six days of in test days, and two post test days, lasting a total of twenty-one days.    
<P>
This was not a hypothesis-driven study.  A 9.5–degree head-up-tilt (HUT) bed rest model, which was the equivalent of 1/6<sup>th</sup> gravity was applied to the lower extremities for periods representing standing.  Several daily task activities, exercise, and other weight-bearing activities, where examined for its feasibility in the simulation of a lunar mission component.
<P>
The bed rest (BR) portion of this study was conducted at the UTMB GCRC for a period of six days.  Subjects remained in a 9.5–degree head-up position to simulate 1/6<sup>th</sup> gravity loading on the feet.  They remained in this position during the bed rest phase of the study (approximately 16 hours each day) except during periods of sleeping (at 0–degrees).  Subjects experienced periods of standing (approximately 35% of the waking day) and sitting (approximately 65% of the waking day).  Sitting was performed in bed at a 9.5–degree angle with a chair-feature incorporated in the bed.  The percentage of time to be sitting was based on foot force data collected as part of the Foot Reaction Forces during Space Flight project.
<P>
The bed design consisted of a modified hospital bed.  Modifications included mounting six linear bearings to the bottom of a sled which are on rails mounted directly on the existing bed frame.  A hinged mattress was on top of the sled.  The mattress was hinged to allow for a seated configuration.  Hard stops were incorporated at both the head end and foot end of the sled to prevent it from sliding off the bed frame in either direction.  Additionally, a counterweight system was incorporated in the design to counterbalance the weight of the sled and mattress.  This was done to allow the subject to get the true 1/6<sup>th</sup> gravity load at the 9.5–degree head up position.
<P>
During LAFS Phase 2 subjects wore knee high stockings at 25–32 mmHg of compression (Jobst® stockings).  It was determined during the first phase of the LAFS study that thigh high stockings with 30–40mmHg of compression while efficacious, were causing discomfort in subjects when bending the knee during the sitting position.  These devices were used to simulate the cardiovascular load of lunar gravity.  The support stockings provided counter pressure on the legs, which helped to prevent the pooling of blood and fluids in the lower extremities.  In lunar gravity, which is only 1/6<sup>th</sup> of the gravity on Earth, there is less likelihood of pooling of blood and fluids in the lower legs and feet due to the decrease of gravitational pull on the body compared to bed rest simulation. 
<P> 
In addition to the Standard Measures testing,one extramural investigator participated during this study:<P>
<LI>Foot Reaction Forces during Bed Rest Analog for Lunar Exploration (Foot_Forces) <BR> (PI: Cromwell, Ronita, Ph.D. NASA Johnson Space Center (JSC), Houston, Texas)</LI> <P>
Daily foot force profiles were analyzed to determine whether appropriate loading was being achieved and maintained without protocol violations.  The objective of this test is to measure the contact force between the subject‘s feet and the foot support surface of the Lunar Gravity Simulator bed while tilted at 9.5–degrees head-up tilt, and confirm that the force is equal to 1/6<sup>th</sup> of the subject‘s 1G body weight (simulated lunar weight).
<P>
Feasibility was determined partially by the subjects‘acceptance of the protocol, based on daily comfort logs.  Daily foot force profiles were analyzed to determine whether appropriate loading is achieved and maintained without protocol violations.  Muscle volumes (from MRI), cardiovascular measures (plasma volume), and any changes in urine calcium will be compared to data from short-duration lunar and shuttle missions for comparability.  MRI data and urine calcium data from all subjects in the first phase of this study are not collected during Phase 2, since sufficient information for proper muscle and bone assessments were determined in the previous study.  Plasma volumes will continue to be performed as more data are needed to verify the plasma volume shift in this lunar model. 
<P>
During the study, subjects were permitted to prop themselves up on their elbows for eating purposes.  Dietary intake sufficient to maintain constant body weight with a balanced intake of macro– and micro–nutrients were planned and supervised by a registered dietician. Food was not brought in through visitors or delivery services.  Subjects were fed a strict weight-maintaining metabolic diet modeled after diets used in previous bed rest studies.  Subjects participated in the simulation of daily tasks, which were performed in bed.  Subjects performed stretch exercises designed to minimize the risk of vascular pathology.  Daily activity and comfort logs were kept by the subject throughout the bed rest phase of the study 
<P>
In order to ensure compliance, subjects were monitored with infrared video cameras at all times while at the GCRC throughout the bed rest portion of the study. Privacy was provided during toileting and bathing as needed.  Test subjects were provided with a bed pan and/ or urinal for toileting purposes, as well as nursing assistance for toileting as needed.  Subjects were able to sponge bathe daily.  In addition, in-bed shampooing, bathing, and showering were provided on a regular basis with nursing assistance.  A UTMB physician was available 24 hours per day to attend to any medical needs.
<P>
Subjects remained in the GCRC for approximately two days after becoming ambulatory.  During the recovery period subjects were allowed to ambulate normally.  Subjects were required to exercise at the suggested reconditioning protocol which was designed specific to each subject‘s needs. 
